60	     could not be partially overridden that way.
61	
62	     Lightness is the one channel that cannot simply be the brand value in both
63	     themes. --accent is used as TEXT in a dozen places, and #2ed8b0 is only
64	     1.51:1 on the #eaeaea surface — invisible. So dark mode carries the brand
65	     color exactly (51% -> #2cd8b0, 7.69:1) while light mode darkens the same
66	     hue until it is legible (32% -> #198a70, 3.56:1), clearing the 3.5:1 the
67	     solver itself targets. Same brand hue in both, only as light as each
68	     surface allows. This is the same reason --brand is documented fill-only.
69	
70	     These apply wherever the solver does not override them: on the shipped
71	     wallpaper, which is brand-locked and deliberately does not tint the UI
72	     with its own hue; on backgrounds with no usable hue (grayscale photos,
73	     "none", near-neutral solids); and in the frame before async image analysis
74	     resolves. In each case applyDerivedAccent clears its inline vars and the
75	     brand default below is what shows through. Any other background — a
76	     gradient, a solid, a user's own upload — still derives and overrides. */
